  "content": {
    "id": "p52-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "$f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2}{x+1}$. Find the value of $c$ if $f'(c)=-3$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$c=-\\dfrac12$ or $-\\dfrac32$.",
      "worked": "Let $u=x^2$ and $v=x+1$, so $u'=2x$ and $v'=1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{2x(x+1)-x^2(1)}{(x+1)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{x^2+2x}{(x+1)^2}\\\\\\frac{c^2+2c}{(c+1)^2}&=-3\\\\c^2+2c&=-3(c+1)^2\\\\c^2+2c&=-3c^2-6c-3\\\\4c^2+8c+3&=0\\\\(2c+1)(2c+3)&=0\\\\c&=-\\frac12\\text{ or }-\\frac32\\end{align*}$$\nBoth values satisfy $c\\ne-1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 58
  },
